### Why does sharding exist?

A single database node has finite disk, memory, CPU, and connections. Replication scales reads but not writes. Sharding scales both writes and data size by distributing rows across many nodes.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class WhySharding {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Single node limits:");
        System.out.println("  Disk: 2 TB -> full");
        System.out.println("  CPU: 96 cores -> saturated on writes");
        System.out.println("  Connections: 5000 -> exhausted");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Replication: scales READS but not WRITES");
        System.out.println("  10 replicas -> 10x read capacity");
        System.out.println("  But still 1 primary -> same write capacity");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Sharding: scales BOTH reads AND writes");
        System.out.println("  4 shards -> 4x write capacity, 4x storage");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Single node limits:
  Disk: 2 TB -> full
  CPU: 96 cores -> saturated on writes
  Connections: 5000 -> exhausted

Replication: scales READS but not WRITES
Sharding: scales BOTH reads AND writes
```

### How does range-based sharding work?

Assign contiguous key ranges to shards. Accounts 1-1M on shard 1, 1M-2M on shard 2.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class RangeSharding {
    public static int getShard(long accountId) {
        if (accountId <= 1_000_000) return 1;
        if (accountId <= 2_000_000) return 2;
        if (accountId <= 3_000_000) return 3;
        return 4;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Account 500000 -> Shard " + getShard(500_000));
        System.out.println("Account 1500000 -> Shard " + getShard(1_500_000));
        System.out.println("Account 3500000 -> Shard " + getShard(3_500_000));
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Pro: range queries hit one shard");
        System.out.println("Con: new accounts always go to latest shard -> HOT SPOT");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Account 500000 -> Shard 1
Account 1500000 -> Shard 2
Account 3500000 -> Shard 4

Pro: range queries hit one shard
Con: new accounts always go to latest shard -> HOT SPOT
```

### How does hash-based sharding work?

Apply hash(key) % N for even distribution. Use consistent hashing to avoid massive remap on resize.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class HashSharding {
    private static final int TOTAL_SHARDS = 4;

    public static int getShard(String accountId) {
        return Math.abs(accountId.hashCode()) % TOTAL_SHARDS;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("SAV2001 -> Shard " + getShard("SAV2001"));
        System.out.println("SAV2002 -> Shard " + getShard("SAV2002"));
        System.out.println("CUR3001 -> Shard " + getShard("CUR3001"));
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Pro: even distribution (no hot spots)");
        System.out.println("Con: range queries scatter across ALL shards");
        System.out.println("Con: adding a shard remaps almost all keys (use consistent hashing)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
SAV2001 -> Shard 2
SAV2002 -> Shard 1
CUR3001 -> Shard 0

Pro: even distribution (no hot spots)
Con: range queries scatter across ALL shards
Con: adding a shard remaps almost all keys (use consistent hashing)
```

### How do the sharding types compare?

| Type | Distribution | Range Queries | Rebalancing | Best For |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Range | Can be uneven (hot spots) | Single shard | Easy (extend range) | Time-series, sequential IDs |
| Hash | Even | Scatter-gather | Hard (modulo remaps) | Most workloads |
| Directory | Flexible (explicit mapping) | Depends on mapping | Easy (update table) | Custom placement needs |
| Geographic | By region (can be uneven) | Within region | Manual | Data-residency compliance |

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class ShardingComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Choosing a sharding type:");
        System.out.println("  Range: when range queries dominate and keys are uniform");
        System.out.println("  Hash: when even distribution matters most");
        System.out.println("  Directory: when you need custom placement or easy rebalancing");
        System.out.println("  Geographic: when data residency or latency is the driver");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Choosing a sharding type:
  Range: when range queries dominate and keys are uniform
  Hash: when even distribution matters most
  Directory: when you need custom placement or easy rebalancing
  Geographic: when data residency or latency is the driver
```

### What are the advantages of sharding?

Horizontal write scalability, fault isolation, and smaller per-shard datasets.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class ShardingAdvantages {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Advantages:");
        System.out.println("  1. Write scalability: 4 shards -> 4x write throughput");
        System.out.println("  2. Data size: each shard holds 25% of data -> fits in memory");
        System.out.println("  3. Fault isolation: shard-1 fails -> only 25% of customers affected");
        System.out.println("  4. Smaller indexes: faster queries per shard");
        System.out.println("  5. Locality: geographic sharding reduces latency");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Advantages:
  1. Write scalability: 4 shards -> 4x write throughput
  2. Data size: each shard holds 25% of data -> fits in memory
  3. Fault isolation: shard-1 fails -> only 25% of customers affected
  4. Smaller indexes: faster queries per shard
  5. Locality: geographic sharding reduces latency
```

### What are the disadvantages of sharding?

Operational complexity, expensive cross-shard queries, hard rebalancing, no cross-shard ACID.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class ShardingDisadvantages {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Disadvantages:");
        System.out.println("  1. Operational complexity: run and monitor many databases");
        System.out.println("  2. Cross-shard queries: scatter-gather to ALL shards -> slow");
        System.out.println("  3. Cross-shard transactions: no single-node ACID -> need Saga");
        System.out.println("  4. Rebalancing: adding shards can move huge amounts of data");
        System.out.println("  5. Joins across shards: impractical -> denormalize");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Rule: shard ONLY when a single well-tuned node with replicas");
        System.out.println("      truly cannot cope. The complexity is real.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Disadvantages:
  1. Operational complexity: run and monitor many databases
  2. Cross-shard queries: scatter-gather to ALL shards -> slow
  3. Cross-shard transactions: no single-node ACID -> need Saga
  4. Rebalancing: adding shards can move huge amounts of data
  5. Joins across shards: impractical -> denormalize

Rule: shard ONLY when a single well-tuned node with replicas
      truly cannot cope.
```

### What is consistent hashing and why does it matter?

Plain modulo remaps almost every key when you add a shard. Consistent hashing remaps only 1/N of keys.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class ConsistentHashing {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Plain modulo: hash(key) % 4");
        System.out.println("  Add shard 5: hash(key) % 5");
        System.out.println("  ~80% of keys remap to different shards -> massive data migration!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Consistent hashing:");
        System.out.println("  Add shard 5: only ~1/5 of keys remap");
        System.out.println("  Much less data to move -> safer, faster");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Plain modulo: hash(key) % 4
  Add shard 5: hash(key) % 5
  ~80% of keys remap to different shards -> massive data migration!

Consistent hashing:
  Add shard 5: only ~1/5 of keys remap
  Much less data to move -> safer, faster
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Shard only when you must. Choose a key that spreads evenly AND keeps common queries on one shard. Co-locate related data.

```text
Senior sharding checklist:
  [x] Shard only when single node + replicas truly cannot cope
  [x] Shard key: high cardinality + common query filter
  [x] Co-locate related data (customer + accounts + transactions)
  [x] Consistent hashing over modulo for growth
  [x] Each shard gets its own primary + replica pair
  [x] Monitor per-shard metrics for data and traffic skew
  [x] Cross-shard transactions: use Saga, not 2PC
```
